  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">EU-UN Spotlight initiative to fight all violence against women and girls (VAWG)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Spotlight Initiative is an ambitious multi-year partnership between the European Union and the United Nations to eliminate all forms of violence against women and girls, herei ncluded traditional harmful practices such as Female Genital Mutilations (FGM) which has a rate in Sierra Leone of 86.1%. The action will be implemented in partnership with national and sub-national authorities. Civil Society Organisations will play a vital role in design and implementation.&amp;nbsp;This initiative builds on lessons learnt from the global EU-UN flagship programme and aims to scale resources, promote evidence-based programmes, and intensify focused action in key areas for the country.&amp;nbsp;It aims directly at advancement of SDG 5 on Gender Equality.The overall objective is that &#xbf;All women and girls in Sierra Leone, particularly the most vulnerable, live a life free of violence and harmful practices, collectively described as violence against women and girls (VAWG).&#xbf;&amp;nbsp;There are four Outcome Areas:1) An enforced enabling institutional, legal and policy environment at the National, District, and Chiefdom levels that protects and safeguards women and girls from VAWG;2) Engaged institutional, social, and community stakeholders at different levels of society working to accelerate change of social norms, attitudes and behaviors for prevention of VAWG and realization of gender equality.3) Scaled-up VAWG response in the form of quality and accessible preventative/curative health services for survivors and improved justice mechanisms to hold perpetrators accountable. 4) capacitated women&#xbf;s CSOs working together to promote gender equality, end VAWG, and support survivors of VAWG through a range of actions at both national and community levels, with skills and resources to sustain these activities. Four Recipient UN agencies will implement the programme, with roles designated according to their expertise, management capacity, and established partner relationships in country, including with key government counterparts and the civil society community. The UN Resident Coordinator in Sierra Leone will have responsibility for coordination and overall programme accountability.&amp;nbsp;Civil Society will have a central role both in design and in the implementation of the programme.&amp;nbsp;</narrative>
  </description>
